;; The GIL and the threads' state (PY6f; PYS10 (a); Python/ceval_gil.c, Python/pystate.c). One Python thread runs
;; at a time: the goroutine holding the GIL. current (frame.go) is its *Thread, swapped on taking the GIL as
;; CPython swaps _PyThreadState_Current, so the object model needs no locks and currentThread() is right in every
;; goroutine that holds it. The GIL is inactive until the first thread starts: the starter becomes its holder then,
;; so a program of one thread only pays the eval breaker's load. A waiter asks the holder to drop it after
;; sys.getswitchinterval() (gil_drop_request); the holder drops it at its next check of the eval breaker (a frame's
;; start, a backward jump: eval.go) and waits until another thread took it (FORCE_SWITCHING). Blocking Go that
;; touches no Python object runs with the GIL released (allowThreads, blockOn: Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS).


